WEBVTT

00:01.001 --> 00:04.921
<i>It seems today that all you see</i>

00:04.922 --> 00:08.174
<i>Is violence in movies and sex on TV</i>

00:08.175 --> 00:11.802
<i>But where are those
good old-fashioned values</i>

00:11.803 --> 00:14.847
<i>On which we used to rely?</i>

00:14.848 --> 00:18.226
<i>Lucky there's a family guy</i>

00:18.227 --> 00:21.604
<i>Lucky there's a man
who positively can do</i>

00:21.605 --> 00:23.189
<i>All the things that make us</i>

00:23.190 --> 00:24.690
<i>Laugh and cry</i>

00:24.691 --> 00:30.155
<i>He's... a... Fam... ily... Guy!</i>

00:35.410 --> 00:37.245
Good evening, Quahog. Our top story:

00:37.246 --> 00:39.914
a local Yankee fan's dream comes true

00:39.915 --> 00:42.416
when he's diagnosed
with Lou Gehrig's disease.

00:42.417 --> 00:43.793
But first...

00:43.794 --> 00:45.836
Hey, the TV went out.

00:45.837 --> 00:47.630
Huh.

00:47.631 --> 00:50.841
Oh, my God, the Wi-Fi is out.

00:58.100 --> 00:59.936
Okay, everyone calm down.

00:59.937 --> 01:01.477
I know how we'll get through this.

01:01.478 --> 01:05.273
This is the story of
the greatest trilogy ever told,

01:05.274 --> 01:07.024
<i>The Mighty Ducks.</i>

01:07.025 --> 01:09.277
It was a time of great nonsense.

01:09.278 --> 01:11.028
Dad, why don't you tell us
how you and Mom

01:11.029 --> 01:12.613
fell in love and got married?

01:12.614 --> 01:14.782
And in go the earbuds.

01:14.783 --> 01:16.617
Terrific episode idea, Meg.

01:16.618 --> 01:18.452
Well, it was the 1990s,

01:18.453 --> 01:21.163
the decade of Viagra
but also Lorena Bobbitt,

01:21.164 --> 01:24.166
so it was kind of
a yay-boo period for penises.

01:24.167 --> 01:26.836
After graduating, your mom
and me moved to the city

01:26.837 --> 01:28.170
with all our friends.

01:33.760 --> 01:36.470
<i>I lived with my best friends,
Quagmire and Cleveland.</i>

01:36.471 --> 01:38.681
<i>We were the kind of friends
who were all good-looking,</i>

01:38.682 --> 01:40.933
<i>except for David Schwimmer.</i>

01:40.934 --> 01:42.476
<i>And your mom lived across the hall</i>

01:42.477 --> 01:44.395
<i>with her friends Bonnie and...</i>

01:44.396 --> 01:45.813
<i>uh, Bonnie.</i>

01:45.814 --> 01:47.315
<i>God, we desperately need to get</i>

01:47.316 --> 01:49.275
<i>more female characters in this show.</i>

01:49.276 --> 01:50.526
<i>I was the Chandler.</i>

01:50.527 --> 01:52.600
<i>Not because of my sarcastic zingers</i>

01:52.601 --> 01:55.323
<i>but because I liked
to take 40 Vicodin a day.</i>

01:55.324 --> 01:56.741
Guys, I've got big news.

01:56.742 --> 01:58.492
Tell 'em, Quagmire.

01:58.493 --> 01:59.994
I got a promotion at Blockbuster!

02:01.747 --> 02:05.041
Gleonard Quagmire,
that is fantastic news!

02:05.042 --> 02:06.876
I know, right?
You know how I always felt

02:06.877 --> 02:08.336
like I was stuck in second gear?

02:08.337 --> 02:09.837
Well, this is a new gear!

02:09.838 --> 02:11.589
Well, congratulations, Glenn!

02:11.590 --> 02:14.050
This calls for a fountain dance.

02:17.054 --> 02:19.555
<i>I was the least graceful
at fountain dancing.</i>

02:19.556 --> 02:21.974
<i>I just swung my arms
and tried to fit in.</i>

02:21.975 --> 02:23.225
<i>It was senseless.
We were in our clothes,</i>

02:23.226 --> 02:24.894
<i>for God's sake. Everyone got wet.</i>

02:24.895 --> 02:26.395
<i>It was cold. You know,</i>

02:26.396 --> 02:27.813
<i>there were originally nine friends,</i>

02:27.814 --> 02:29.446
<i>but three of 'em died of pneumonia.</i>

02:29.447 --> 02:32.735
<i>I'm still haunted
by Chad's shivering last words,</i>

02:32.736 --> 02:35.237
<i>"Don't let Monica get Botox."</i>

02:38.992 --> 02:42.328
<i>We also hung out at
our favorite cafГ©, Perk Place,</i>

02:42.329 --> 02:44.205
<i>where we would all sweat like crazy</i>

02:44.206 --> 02:45.915
<i>'cause everyone in the '90s
wore turtlenecks,</i>

02:45.916 --> 02:48.167
<i>vests, and Jamiroquai hats.</i>

02:48.168 --> 02:49.960
Lois, you look down. What's wrong?

02:49.961 --> 02:52.755
Brad Pitt broke up with me
in a pretty public way.

02:52.756 --> 02:55.424
I just don't know
if I'll ever date again.

02:55.425 --> 02:57.885
Wait, I thought you and Dad
dated in high school.

02:57.886 --> 02:59.136
Did you break up?

02:59.137 --> 03:01.263
Uh-uh, story's not over, Meg.
Settle down.

03:01.264 --> 03:03.641
Your father and I had dated
for a few years,

03:03.642 --> 03:05.476
but I didn't know
if it was going anywhere.

03:05.477 --> 03:08.938
So we decided to take a break
but remained close friends.

03:08.939 --> 03:11.899
I actually briefly dated one
of our roommates, Barry.

03:11.900 --> 03:14.110
He was one of them
what the fountain took.

03:14.111 --> 03:17.113
My father had planned out
my whole life for me.

03:17.114 --> 03:19.824
<i>He wanted me to marry one
of Quahog's up-and-comers,</i>

03:19.825 --> 03:21.492
<i>Tom Tucker, Elmer Hartman,</i>

03:21.493 --> 03:23.160
<i>Seamus or Billy Zane.</i>

03:23.161 --> 03:25.955
<i>It's meeting the man of my dreams</i>

03:25.956 --> 03:29.083
<i>And then meeting his beautiful wife</i>

03:29.084 --> 03:30.793
<i>And isn't it ironic</i>

03:30.794 --> 03:32.420
<i>Don't you think?</i>

03:32.421 --> 03:35.965
<i>It's like rain...</i>

03:35.966 --> 03:38.259
<i>On your wedding day.</i>

03:38.260 --> 03:42.763
<i>Alanis Morissette was America's
sweetheart in the '90s.</i>

03:42.764 --> 03:45.266
<i>She went on to become one
of the CDs in the garage.</i>

03:45.267 --> 03:47.435
<i>Wait, Seamus had arms and legs?</i>

03:47.436 --> 03:49.645
<i>Chris, I'm not sure
you have the green light</i>

03:49.646 --> 03:51.147
<i>to just chime in whenever.</i>

03:51.148 --> 03:54.817
Lois, let's go take a midnight
swim at Sharkbite Cove.

03:54.818 --> 03:56.652
Oh, sounds wonderful, Seamus,

03:56.653 --> 03:58.446
but I have other plans.

03:58.447 --> 04:01.741
Okay, well, I'm definitely going.

04:05.662 --> 04:07.455
Peter, why don't you admit it?

04:07.456 --> 04:09.206
You still got the hots for Lois.

04:09.207 --> 04:10.207
It's obvious.

04:10.208 --> 04:12.293
Nah, we're broken up.
We're just friends now.

04:13.628 --> 04:15.504
<i>Looking back, I realize he was right.</i>

04:15.505 --> 04:17.840
<i>But the '90s was an exciting time,</i>

04:17.841 --> 04:20.885
<i>and we wanted to experience
all it had to offer.</i>

04:20.886 --> 04:22.511
Hey, guys, there's a great concert

04:22.512 --> 04:24.555
in World Trade Center Plaza
this weekend!

04:24.556 --> 04:25.973
Really? Who's playing?

04:25.974 --> 04:28.017
They got Korn, Cranberries and Cake.

04:28.018 --> 04:29.977
Oh, that sounds delicious.
But who are the bands?

04:29.978 --> 04:31.312
They're opening with Cake.

04:31.313 --> 04:33.397
Wait, shouldn't cake be last?
It's a dessert.

04:33.398 --> 04:35.733
Korn is last.
Korn is a bigger draw than Cake.

04:35.734 --> 04:37.693
Corn is not a bigger draw than cake.

04:37.694 --> 04:39.361
- Cake is delicious.
- "No Doubt"?

04:39.362 --> 04:41.155
Well, I'm glad
you agree with me, Quagmire,

04:41.156 --> 04:43.657
but that still doesn't address
this issue of the order.

04:43.658 --> 04:45.743
My last show was a Weezer concert.

04:45.744 --> 04:48.037
Well, that's 'cause
everyone smokes nowadays.

04:48.038 --> 04:50.164
What band did we see last time
at the Palladium?

04:50.165 --> 04:51.207
It was, uh, Blur.

04:51.208 --> 04:52.750
Yeah, I can't remember, either.

04:52.751 --> 04:55.377
<i>It was a very confusing time in music.</i>

04:55.378 --> 04:57.338
<i>Okay, time for another scene.</i>

04:57.339 --> 04:59.757
<i>Let's transition
with a '90s guitar sting</i>

04:59.758 --> 05:01.760
<i>that's way too long.</i>

05:11.812 --> 05:13.896
<i>- Dad, what...
- H-Hang on, Chris.</i>

05:13.897 --> 05:15.064
<i>It's not done yet.</i>

05:22.806 --> 05:24.073
<i>Okay, there we go.</i>

05:24.074 --> 05:25.950
<i>What'd you do for a living, Dad?</i>

05:25.951 --> 05:27.701
<i>What any of us
did never really mattered.</i>

05:27.702 --> 05:28.869
<i>It was just "vague business."</i>

05:28.870 --> 05:30.746
<i>But despite having broken up,</i>

05:30.747 --> 05:32.748
<i>I would sometimes sneak away
to the Internet</i>

05:32.749 --> 05:34.291
<i>to go flirt with your mom.</i>

05:34.292 --> 05:36.627
Well, I'm gonna go move my bangs
with my pinky

05:36.628 --> 05:38.046
in front of the mirror.

05:39.297 --> 05:41.090
<i>- Welcome!
- Shh, shh, shh, shh!</i>

05:41.091 --> 05:43.092
<i>- You've got mail!
- Okay, good, but quiet.</i>

05:43.093 --> 05:44.426
<i>You've got male porn!</i>

05:44.427 --> 05:45.970
Shh, shh, shh!

05:45.971 --> 05:48.889
Peter, quit trying to flirt with
that clearly girlfriend of yours

05:48.890 --> 05:51.267
and come watch giant box TV with us!

05:51.268 --> 05:53.143
<i>We all watched The X-Files,</i>

05:53.144 --> 05:55.563
<i>which would be Fox's last hit show.</i>

05:55.564 --> 05:58.482
<i>And it was the heyday
of the multi-camera sitcom,</i>

05:58.483 --> 06:00.568
<i>and Paul Reiser, its king,</i>

06:00.569 --> 06:01.819
<i>was must-see.</i>

06:01.820 --> 06:04.238
Perhaps I'll try this yogurt.

06:04.239 --> 06:06.448
That's been in there since our wedding.

06:06.449 --> 06:09.785
Perhaps... I won't.

06:14.207 --> 06:15.499
Peter, admit it.

06:15.500 --> 06:17.293
You're still in love with Lois.

06:17.294 --> 06:19.420
Yeah, every time you see her,
you go, "Schwing!"

06:19.421 --> 06:21.130
Which is, like, the sound

06:21.131 --> 06:23.007
of being lifted by a boner, I think.

06:23.008 --> 06:25.301
Yeah, but we decided
we wanted to see other people.

06:25.302 --> 06:28.095
That's only 'cause she got tired
of waiting around for you.

06:28.096 --> 06:29.597
Look, she loves you,

06:29.598 --> 06:31.849
but she ain't gonna wait around forever.

06:31.850 --> 06:32.975
<i>I knew they were right.</i>

06:32.976 --> 06:34.351
<i>Your mom was the only one</i>

06:34.352 --> 06:36.979
<i>I wanted to spend
the rest of my life with.</i>

06:36.980 --> 06:38.856
<i>So I saved my money,
and, a few months later,</i>

06:38.857 --> 06:40.190
<i>I bought a ring.</i>

06:40.191 --> 06:43.404
So, did you enjoy your pesto with pesto?

06:43.405 --> 06:45.989
Does this smile give you your answer?

06:47.699 --> 06:50.284
Lois, there's something
I want to ask you.

06:50.285 --> 06:52.912
Will you marry me?

06:52.913 --> 06:55.456
Oh, Peter, I'm-I'm so sorry, but...

06:55.457 --> 06:57.917
I'm already engaged to Tom Tucker.

06:57.918 --> 07:00.044
Passed over for Tom Tucker?

07:00.045 --> 07:01.795
That's terrible.

07:01.796 --> 07:04.298
What do you think of that,
fellow '90s star Joey Lawrence?

07:04.299 --> 07:06.300
- Meh.
- Oh. Huh.

07:06.301 --> 07:08.302
Okay, well, what would you think
if I told you

07:08.303 --> 07:10.471
that Blossom would completely
drop out of show business

07:10.472 --> 07:12.389
to get a PhD in neuroscience

07:12.390 --> 07:15.059
